<?xml version="1.0"?>

<!DOCTYPE suppressions PUBLIC
    "-//Checkstyle//DTD SuppressionXpathFilter Experimental Configuration 1.2//EN"
    "https://checkstyle.org/dtds/suppressions_1_2_xpath_experimental.dtd">

<!--
  Shared between every ruleset in configs/checkstyle/ (referenced via ${config_loc}/../_shared/...)
  so the Lombok-specific exceptions below have one source of truth instead of drifting copies.

  Lombok generates its members (getters, setters, constructors, equals/hashCode/toString, builders,
  etc.) after Checkstyle parses the source, so Checkstyle never sees generated code and almost never
  conflicts with it. What follows covers the few cases where a check draws a conclusion from what the
  developer's own source text does NOT say, and Lombok is what silently supplies the missing part:

    - @UtilityClass makes every member static, marks the class itself final, and adds a private
      constructor, but none of "static", "final", or the constructor ever appear in source (see
      https://projectlombok.org/features/experimental/UtilityClass). RequireThis would otherwise
      demand "this." on what become static members; FinalClass would otherwise demand "final" on
      the class; DesignForExtension would otherwise demand every method be proven safe to override
      on what becomes a non-extensible, fully static class. A field Lombok will make static is also
      still classified as MemberName (instance-field naming), not StaticVariableName, since that
      classification comes from the "static" keyword in source too - a real divergence here, not
      just a theoretical one: MemberName is configured to require 2+ characters while
      StaticVariableName is left at Checkstyle's default, which allows 1+, so a single-character
      field name is wrongly rejected unless MemberName is suppressed for it too. (Lombok never adds
      "final" to a @UtilityClass field, only "static", so there is no equivalent ConstantName
      concern.)
    - @Value makes fields private and final and the class itself final, without ever writing those
      keywords in source. @FieldDefaults - the annotation @Value is implemented in terms of - can
      be applied on its own to set the same field-level defaults (`makeFinal`, `level`), but never
      touches the class itself: a bare @FieldDefaults class is never made final by Lombok, so
      FinalClass is suppressed for @Value only, not for @FieldDefaults
      (https://projectlombok.org/features/experimental/FieldDefaults).
    - lombok.val infers the type of, and forces the finality of, a local variable without the
      "final" keyword ever appearing in source. Checkstyle classifies a variable as final or not
      from that keyword alone, so it also checks a `val` variable's name against
      LocalVariableName's (non-final) pattern rather than LocalFinalVariableName's - in this
      ruleset a real divergence, not just a theoretical one: LocalVariableName is configured to
      require 2+ characters while LocalFinalVariableName is left at Checkstyle's default, which
      allows 1+, so a single-character `val` name is wrongly rejected unless LocalVariableName is
      suppressed for it too.

  HideUtilityClassConstructor is deliberately NOT suppressed here: since Checkstyle 10.20 it has a
  built-in `ignoreAnnotatedBy` property, configured directly on the module in checkstyle.xml, which
  is more precise and robust than an xpath match (see checkstyle.xml in each ruleset directory).

  Every query below is anchored on CLASS_DEF's own annotation rather than any node carrying a
  same-named annotation. This matters because Spring's unrelated field/parameter-level
  @org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value can never legally target a class (it has no
  ElementType.TYPE in its @Target), so anchoring on CLASS_DEF avoids also suppressing checks for
  classes that merely inject a Spring-managed property with the same-named but unrelated annotation.
  This does not help against a fully-qualified `@lombok.Value` / `@lombok.experimental.UtilityClass`
  reference in place of the plain imported name; that is a known, accepted limitation of matching
  annotations by simple name, shared with essentially every xpath-suppression file in the wild.
-->
<suppressions>
    <!-- @UtilityClass: every member becomes static, the class becomes final, and it gets a
         synthesized private constructor. RequireThis would otherwise require "this." inside what
         becomes a fully static class; FinalClass would otherwise demand the "final" keyword;
         DesignForExtension would otherwise demand every method be proven safe to override on a
         class that Lombok has already made both final and fully static. -->
    <suppress-xpath checks="RequireThis"
                    query="//CLASS_DEF[./MODIFIERS/ANNOTATION/IDENT[@text='UtilityClass']]/descendant-or-self::node()"/>
    <suppress-xpath checks="FinalClass"
                    query="//CLASS_DEF[./MODIFIERS/ANNOTATION/IDENT[@text='UtilityClass']]/descendant-or-self::node()"/>
    <suppress-xpath checks="DesignForExtension"
                    query="//CLASS_DEF[./MODIFIERS/ANNOTATION/IDENT[@text='UtilityClass']]/descendant-or-self::node()"/>
    <suppress-xpath checks="MemberName"
                    query="//CLASS_DEF[./MODIFIERS/ANNOTATION/IDENT[@text='UtilityClass']]/descendant-or-self::node()"/>

    <!-- @Value / @FieldDefaults: both can make fields private and/or final without those keywords
         ever appearing in source, so both are matched for the two field-level checks. Only @Value
         also makes the class itself final - @FieldDefaults never does, on its own, regardless of
         its makeFinal/level properties - so FinalClass below matches @Value alone. -->
    <suppress-xpath checks="VisibilityModifier"
                    query="//CLASS_DEF[./MODIFIERS/ANNOTATION/IDENT[@text='Value' or @text='FieldDefaults']]/descendant-or-self::node()"/>
    <suppress-xpath checks="MutableException"
                    query="//CLASS_DEF[./MODIFIERS/ANNOTATION/IDENT[@text='Value' or @text='FieldDefaults']]/descendant-or-self::node()"/>
    <suppress-xpath checks="FinalClass"
                    query="//CLASS_DEF[./MODIFIERS/ANNOTATION/IDENT[@text='Value']]/descendant-or-self::node()"/>

    <!-- lombok.val: infers the type and forces the immutability of a local variable, but the
         "final" keyword this check wants is never written. Native Java `var` is deliberately NOT
         matched here - unlike lombok.val, it carries no immutability guarantee to rely on. Both
         FinalLocalVariable and LocalVariableName are suppressed: the variable is checked as
         non-final by both, since Checkstyle's final/non-final classification comes from the
         "final" keyword in source, which `val` never writes. -->
    <suppress-xpath checks="FinalLocalVariable"
                    query="//VARIABLE_DEF[./TYPE/IDENT[@text='val']]/descendant-or-self::node()"/>
    <suppress-xpath checks="LocalVariableName"
                    query="//VARIABLE_DEF[./TYPE/IDENT[@text='val']]/descendant-or-self::node()"/>
</suppressions>
